      "writtenOn": "2023-09-26T16:32:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Apologies, I misread the code 😊. I retract my first comment, though this has turned into an interesting thread!\n\nAs Andrew states, the HMAC key here is not related to Open Profile for DICE. In fact, I don\u0027t think we have any specification at all for the HMAC key. (Perhaps a documentation miss in the HAL docs?)\n\nI think the solution in the patch is actually a good one (if not ideal). The security of the generated keys is determined by the current DICE chain. Deriving the HMAC key from the CDI effectively binds the keys to the current DICE chain.\n\n\u003e giving the HMAC key the same stability properties as the key that will be used to wrap the private keys would be good consistency.\n\nI disagree with this point, because RKP keys that are certified when booting an older bootchain must remain valid for signing operations on updated bootchains. (Note: this requirement is likely to change in the future. It would be desirable for security version updates to _require_ fresh KA keys/certs.)\n\nHowever, there\u0027s not a valid case for a key to be generated by an older bootchain then included in a CSR on a newer bootchain. That combo could lead to some interesting attacks; e.g. generate exfiltratable keys on a vulnerable boot chain, patch, then get KA certs indicating the system has patched (presuming we add such information to the X.509 extension in the future).",
      "parentUuid": "597fb015_fbb1c9e7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 29,
        "endLine": 39,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "cc3be25dae064649c52190d4dd5bca3766bf03d5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
